 "note": [
  "Scripture source: the Indian Revised Version (IRV Hindi, CC BY-SA 4.0, Bridge Connectivity Solutions) — the Bible language_seed.py records for hi — fetched as USX from the ebible mirror (gracious-tech/fetch_collection, bibles/hin_irv/usx), all 66 books / 31,104 verses, with section-heading paras, notes and trailing editorial cross-references stripped. Every span marked mined was lifted from that text with a script, never typed.",
  "Chesterton quotes the KJV almost always without citing it, and mostly bends it into his own sentence (parody, re-personing, a phrase inside an argument). So most rows are self_rendered: the translator spliced IRV fragments where they fit and adjusted the grammar around them, which makes the sentence ours, not the Bible's. A row is mined only when the translator reported the quotation verbatim AND a run of 12+ characters of that IRV verse is a byte substring of the block. Unquoted echoes and bare names (the New Jerusalem, Armageddon, 'the image of God', camel and needle) are prose and are not recorded. block_index is per chapter (</p>|</li>|</blockquote>|</h*> split); chapter_order names the chapter.",
  "Editorial items for the reviewer: Acts 26:28 is self-rendered on purpose — the IRV turns Agrippa's line into a question and loses the 'almost' Chesterton's joke rests on. Isaiah 11:6 is the popular misquote 'the lion shall lie down with the lamb', which the IRV verse does not contain; it is rendered as Chesterton wrote it. Song of Songs 6:10: Chesterton swaps the sun and the moon; mirrored. Ezekiel 1:22 keeps 'crystal' as स्फटिक where the IRV has बर्फ, because the paragraph is about glass. Luke 10:27 supplies the IRV wording for 'Thou shalt love the Lord thy God' (Matthew 22:37's IRV wording does not fit the sentence)."
 ],
